2026-05-03 10:45:12.495 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0857, technique_name=System Firmware, technique_description=System firmware on modern assets is often designed with an update feature. Older device firmware may be factory installed and require special reprograming equipment. When available, the firmware update feature enables vendors to remotely patch bugs and perform upgrades. Device firmware updates are often delegated to the user and may be done using a software update package. It may also be possible to perform this task over the network. An adversary may exploit the firmware update feature on accessible devices to upload malicious or out-of-date firmware. Malicious modification of device firmware may provide an adversary with root access to a device, given firmware is one of the lowest programming abstraction layers., control_name=Code signing, control_description=Code signing serves as a critical cryptographic security control within Industrial Control Systems (ICS) and Operational Technology (OT), primarily designed to enforce the integrity and authenticity of firmware, software updates, and programmable logic. By employing asymmetric cryptographic primitives, this mechanism enables end-devices to verify that ingested code remains untampered and originates from a legitimate source, thereby precluding the execution of unauthorized or malicious artifacts. This control framework encompasses both external signatures, which are provided by Original Equipment Manufacturers (OEMs) to attest to the provenance of vendor-supplied software, and internal signatures, which are applied by the asset owner to certify site-specific configurations or custom-developed ladder logic following internal security validation. Consequently, code signing mitigates supply chain vulnerabilities and protects the operational environment from adversarial logic injections by establishing an immutable chain of trust across the asset's lifecycle.}
2026-05-03 10:45:17.190 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Code signing provides a high degree of prevention against malicious firmware uploads (T0857) by cryptographically verifying that firmware originates from legitimate sources and has not been tampered with. The control directly addresses the core attack vector by ensuring that devices will reject unsigned or incorrectly signed firmware, which is fundamental to preventing adversaries from uploading malicious firmware. In a best-in-class deployment with proper implementation across the OT environment, this control would prevent the majority of firmware-based attacks (estimated 75-90% of vectors). However, the rating is not 5 because several edge cases and implementation gaps can still allow attacks: (1) if firmware update mechanisms lack proper certificate pinning or use weak cryptographic validation, an attacker with compromised credentials or through supply chain compromise could sign malicious code; (2) legacy or air-gapped systems may not enforce signature verification at all; (3) downgrade attacks may be possible if anti-rollback protections are not properly implemented alongside code signing; (4) physical attacks or out-of-band firmware update mechanisms (e.g., JTAG, serial interfaces) may bypass cryptographic verification entirely; (5) implementation flaws in the verification process itself could be exploited; and (6) compromise of the signing infrastructure or key material could enable legitimate-appearing malicious firmware. Code signing is architecturally sound and operationally effective for most standard firmware update scenarios, making it a strong preventive control, but cannot address all possible attack vectors or implementation vulnerabilities without complementary controls."}
2026-05-03 10:45:17.190 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0839, technique_name=Module Firmware, technique_description=Adversaries may install malicious or vulnerable firmware onto modular hardware devices. Control system devices often contain modular hardware devices. These devices may have their own set of firmware that is separate from the firmware of the main control system equipment. This technique is similar to System Firmware, but is conducted on other system components that may not have the same capabilities or level of integrity checking. Although it results in a device re-image, malicious device firmware may provide persistent access to remaining devices. An easy point of access for an adversary is the Ethernet card, which may have its own CPU, RAM, and operating system. The adversary may attack and likely exploit the computer on an Ethernet card., control_name=Code signing, control_description=Code signing serves as a critical cryptographic security control within Industrial Control Systems (ICS) and Operational Technology (OT), primarily designed to enforce the integrity and authenticity of firmware, software updates, and programmable logic. By employing asymmetric cryptographic primitives, this mechanism enables end-devices to verify that ingested code remains untampered and originates from a legitimate source, thereby precluding the execution of unauthorized or malicious artifacts. This control framework encompasses both external signatures, which are provided by Original Equipment Manufacturers (OEMs) to attest to the provenance of vendor-supplied software, and internal signatures, which are applied by the asset owner to certify site-specific configurations or custom-developed ladder logic following internal security validation. Consequently, code signing mitigates supply chain vulnerabilities and protects the operational environment from adversarial logic injections by establishing an immutable chain of trust across the asset's lifecycle.}
2026-05-03 10:45:21.898 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Code signing provides strong prevention against Module Firmware attacks by establishing cryptographic verification of firmware authenticity and integrity before execution. The control directly addresses the core attack vector by ensuring that any firmware loaded onto modular hardware components (such as Ethernet cards, network modules, or other peripheral devices) must be cryptographically signed by a trusted source. This prevents adversaries from successfully installing malicious or vulnerable firmware, as unsigned or improperly signed firmware would be rejected at the verification stage. The asymmetric cryptographic approach ensures that attackers cannot forge signatures without access to the private keys held by legitimate vendors or asset owners. However, the rating of 4 rather than 5 reflects several practical limitations: (1) Legacy or specialized modular components may not support code signing verification, leaving them vulnerable; (2) Devices with poor or no secure boot mechanisms may allow firmware installation to bypass signing checks; (3) Vulnerabilities in the signature verification implementation itself could be exploited; (4) Supply chain compromises affecting vendor signing keys represent an edge case where signed malicious firmware could still be deployed; (5) Some modular devices may lack the computational resources to perform robust cryptographic verification. In a best-in-class ICS environment with comprehensive code signing enforcement across all modular hardware components and proper key management, this control effectively prevents 75-90% of Module Firmware attacks, stopping skilled attackers from arbitrary malicious firmware installation while remaining vulnerable only in specific edge cases involving implementation flaws or supply chain compromise."}
2026-05-03 10:45:21.898 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0873, technique_name=Project File Infection, technique_description=Adversaries may attempt to infect project files with malicious code. These project files may consist of objects, program organization units, variables such as tags, documentation, and other configurations needed for PLC programs to function. Using built in functions of the engineering software, adversaries may be able to download an infected program to a PLC in the operating environment enabling further Execution and Persistence techniques. Adversaries may export their own code into project files with conditions to execute at specific intervals. Malicious programs allow adversaries control of all aspects of the process enabled by the PLC., control_name=Code signing, control_description=Code signing serves as a critical cryptographic security control within Industrial Control Systems (ICS) and Operational Technology (OT), primarily designed to enforce the integrity and authenticity of firmware, software updates, and programmable logic. By employing asymmetric cryptographic primitives, this mechanism enables end-devices to verify that ingested code remains untampered and originates from a legitimate source, thereby precluding the execution of unauthorized or malicious artifacts. This control framework encompasses both external signatures, which are provided by Original Equipment Manufacturers (OEMs) to attest to the provenance of vendor-supplied software, and internal signatures, which are applied by the asset owner to certify site-specific configurations or custom-developed ladder logic following internal security validation. Consequently, code signing mitigates supply chain vulnerabilities and protects the operational environment from adversarial logic injections by establishing an immutable chain of trust across the asset's lifecycle.}
2026-05-03 10:45:27.220 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Code signing provides strong prevention of Project File Infection by establishing a cryptographic chain of trust that validates the integrity and authenticity of code before execution. When properly implemented, code signing prevents execution of infected project files through several mechanisms: (1) Detection of tampering - any malicious modifications to project files will invalidate the cryptographic signature, preventing execution; (2) Source verification - only code signed by trusted entities (OEMs or authorized internal developers) will execute, blocking adversary-injected code; (3) Multi-layer protection - both external (OEM) and internal (site-specific) signatures create overlapping validation checkpoints. The control directly addresses the core attack vector where adversaries modify project files and download them to PLCs by ensuring that modified or unsigned malicious programs will fail signature verification. This prevents the 'download an infected program to a PLC' step described in the technique. However, the rating is 4 rather than 5 because: (1) Signature verification must be enforced at both engineering workstation and PLC device levels - incomplete enforcement at either point creates bypass opportunities; (2) Legacy or non-compliant devices may not support code signing verification; (3) Compromised signing keys or weakly protected private keys could allow adversaries to generate valid signatures for malicious code; (4) Implementation gaps where engineering software may not validate signatures before allowing program modifications; (5) Supply chain risks if OEM signing infrastructure itself is compromised. In a best-in-class deployment with comprehensive enforcement across all engineering tools and target devices, code signing prevents the vast majority (75-90%) of project file infection attempts."}
2026-05-03 10:45:27.220 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0862, technique_name=Supply Chain Compromise, technique_description=Adversaries may perform supply chain compromise to gain control systems environment access by means of infected products, software, and workflows. Supply chain compromise is the manipulation of products, such as devices or software, or their delivery mechanisms before receipt by the end consumer. Adversary compromise of these products and mechanisms is done for the goal of data or system compromise, once infected products are introduced to the target environment. Supply chain compromise can occur at all stages of the supply chain, from manipulation of development tools and environments to manipulation of developed products and tools distribution mechanisms., control_name=Code signing, control_description=Code signing serves as a critical cryptographic security control within Industrial Control Systems (ICS) and Operational Technology (OT), primarily designed to enforce the integrity and authenticity of firmware, software updates, and programmable logic. By employing asymmetric cryptographic primitives, this mechanism enables end-devices to verify that ingested code remains untampered and originates from a legitimate source, thereby precluding the execution of unauthorized or malicious artifacts. This control framework encompasses both external signatures, which are provided by Original Equipment Manufacturers (OEMs) to attest to the provenance of vendor-supplied software, and internal signatures, which are applied by the asset owner to certify site-specific configurations or custom-developed ladder logic following internal security validation. Consequently, code signing mitigates supply chain vulnerabilities and protects the operational environment from adversarial logic injections by establishing an immutable chain of trust across the asset's lifecycle.}
2026-05-03 10:45:32.753 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Code signing provides strong prevention against supply chain compromise by establishing cryptographic verification of software and firmware authenticity and integrity. This control directly addresses a significant portion of supply chain compromise vectors by ensuring that only legitimately signed code from trusted sources (OEMs or internal developers) can be executed on ICS devices. When properly implemented with best-in-class coverage, code signing prevents execution of maliciously modified products and software during the supply chain, detecting tampering that occurs before delivery to the end consumer.\n\nHowever, the control does not achieve a perfect 5 rating due to several limiting factors: (1) Code signing relies on the assumption that the signing key itself has not been compromised at the source—if an attacker compromises an OEM's signing infrastructure, malicious code can still be legitimately signed; (2) The control does not prevent compromise of the development tools and environments used to create the code before signing occurs; (3) It requires proper implementation and enforcement across all devices and update mechanisms, and misconfigurations or legacy devices that don't enforce code signature validation create gaps; (4) Supply chain compromise can occur through non-code vectors (e.g., physical tampering, manipulated configurations, or compromise of delivery mechanisms like update servers) which code signing alone does not address; (5) The control depends on proper key management and revocation procedures, which can be circumvented if not rigorously maintained.\n\nCode signing is sufficient to prevent 75-90% of typical software/firmware-based supply chain compromise vectors in well-managed OT environments and will stop most automated exploitation and unskilled attackers, but determined adversaries with access to signing keys or who exploit non-code supply chain pathways can still succeed."}
