2026-05-05 17:48:55.156 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"Network-based firewalls provide moderate prevention against exploitation of remote services in ICS environments. The control offers several relevant protective mechanisms: (1) Port/protocol filtering can block access to vulnerable remote services by restricting traffic to known-good sources and destinations, (2) network segmentation via firewalls limits lateral movement and access to critical systems, and (3) stateful and protocol-aware firewalls can detect and block suspicious communication patterns associated with exploitation attempts. However, the control has significant limitations: (1) it cannot prevent exploitation if the vulnerable service operates on an allowed port/protocol combination, (2) it does not address zero-day vulnerabilities or non-standard exploitation vectors that bypass firewall rules, (3) it cannot validate the legitimacy of allowed traffic or detect sophisticated multi-stage attacks that appear as normal traffic, (4) it relies on proper configuration and maintenance of rules, which is often inconsistent in OT environments, and (5) it does not provide patch management or vulnerability remediation. The control is most effective when combined with strict allowlisting policies and proper network segmentation, making it sufficient to prevent approximately 50-75% of typical exploitation attempts in well-designed networks, while skilled attackers with knowledge of allowed services or insider threats can still succeed. This places it squarely in the moderate prevention category."}
