2026-05-05 17:51:43.394 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"Network-based firewalls provide moderate prevention against spoofed network traffic in ICS environments. Here's the detailed analysis:\n\nStrengths:\n1. Stateful inspection capabilities can detect and block anomalous traffic patterns that violate established connection states, preventing some spoofing attempts where attackers use non-standard connection sequences.\n2. Protocol-aware and ICS-specific firewalls can validate traffic against known legitimate protocol behaviors, blocking spoofed packets that violate protocol specifications.\n3. Access control policies (allowlists/denylists) can restrict communication between specific source-destination pairs, preventing spoofed traffic from unauthorized sources from reaching intended targets.\n4. Network segmentation enforced by firewalls limits the scope of lateral movement even if spoofing occurs, preventing spoofed traffic from crossing security boundaries.\n\nLimitations:\n1. Simple stateless firewalls cannot prevent spoofing at all, as they only filter based on static rules without validating packet legitimacy.\n2. Firewalls cannot reliably verify the true identity of traffic sources if the underlying network infrastructure supports IP spoofing - they filter based on layer 3/4 headers which can be forged.\n3. Spoofed traffic that conforms to allowlisted source-destination pairs and legitimate protocol specifications will pass through firewall inspection.\n4. Advanced spoofing techniques (e.g., DNS spoofing, ARP spoofing, MAC spoofing) operate at layers below traditional firewall inspection or require specific anti-spoofing features not universally implemented.\n5. Zero-day protocols or legitimate-looking but malicious traffic patterns may evade protocol-aware filtering.\n\nThe control stops 50-75% of spoofing vectors in typical OT environments (those attempting to cross segmentation boundaries or violate access control policies), making it sufficient against unskilled attackers and partial automated exploitation attempts, but insufficient against skilled attackers employing sophisticated spoofing techniques that comply with firewall rulesets."}
