2026-05-05 17:52:06.258 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"Network-based firewalls provide partial prevention against impersonation attacks in ICS environments. Their primary strength lies in controlling which traffic is allowed between network segments and enforcing access control policies based on static identifiers (source/destination IP addresses, ports, protocols). This can prevent some impersonation vectors by restricting which devices can communicate with specific systems.\n\nHowever, firewalls have significant limitations against impersonation:\n\n1. **IP/MAC spoofing vulnerability**: Standard firewalls operate at Layers 3-4 and cannot reliably distinguish between legitimate and spoofed network addresses. An attacker can impersonate a trusted device by simply using its IP address, which firewalls cannot prevent without additional mechanisms like DHCP snooping or ARP inspection.\n\n2. **Protocol-level impersonation**: Firewalls cannot detect when an attacker uses legitimate credentials or valid protocol sequences to impersonate an authorized user or device. They lack visibility into application-layer authentication mechanisms.\n\n3. **Trust-based bypass**: If a firewall allows traffic from a compromised but \"legitimate\" device, impersonation from that device cannot be detected by the firewall alone.\n\n4. **Limited effectiveness against insider threats**: Impersonation from within already-trusted network segments bypasses firewall protections.\n\nThe control provides moderate value only when combined with strict network segmentation (preventing impersonation across trust boundaries) and protocol-aware filtering (rejecting malformed traffic). In a typical OT environment with proper segmentation, firewalls might prevent 25-50% of impersonation attacks by controlling which devices can reach which systems, but they cannot prevent impersonation attacks from within trusted zones or at the application layer. A moderately skilled attacker