2026-05-31 15:00:48.727 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Peripheral device control provides a high degree of prevention against the Autorun Image technique. The technique specifically requires adversaries to mount removable media (USB drives, disk images) to a target device and rely on AutoRun/AutoPlay functionality to execute malicious code. Peripheral device control directly addresses both critical components: (1) it restricts or prevents the attachment of removable storage devices through allowlisting, disabling ports, physical blockers, and locked enclosures, and (2) it can enforce read-only access and other restrictions that prevent automatic execution. In a properly implemented environment with comprehensive coverage, this control would prevent the vast majority of Autorun Image attacks by making it difficult or impossible to introduce removable media carrying malicious payloads. The control is not rated 5 because some edge cases may persist: legacy devices with embedded firmware may be difficult to fully restrict, virtual environments might bypass physical controls, and highly privileged attackers with physical access might circumvent port blockers or sealed enclosures through destructive means. Additionally, if AutoRun is still enabled on systems where removable media attachment cannot be completely prevented, the attack could still succeed. However, for 75-90% of vectors in a typical OT environment, this control effectively prevents the technique by eliminating the primary attack vector (introduction of removable media)."}
