2026-05-31 17:29:37.802 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Removal of Unused Applications and Features provides strong prevention against T0819 (Exploit Public-Facing Application) by significantly reducing the attack surface that adversaries can target. This control directly addresses the technique's core mechanism: exploiting internet-facing applications and services.\n\nStrengths of this control against T0819:\n1. By inventorying and disabling/removing unnecessary applications and services, the control eliminates potential entry points that adversaries scan for using tools like Shodan or port scanners.\n2. It reduces exposure of vulnerable control protocol ports and remote access services that aren't operationally required, preventing abuse even if vulnerabilities exist.\n3. The control applies comprehensively across all ICS assets (engineering workstations, HMIs, historians, field devices), limiting multiple attack vectors.\n4. Removing applications entirely is more reliable than patching, as noted in the control description, and prevents zero-day exploitation of unused services.\n\nLimitations preventing a rating of 5:\n1. Some public-facing applications are intentionally exposed for legitimate remote management purposes and cannot be removed, leaving them as potential targets.\n2. The control requires accurate inventory and thorough understanding of what is truly 'unused' - misclassification or incomplete inventory can leave exploitable services enabled.\n3. Even with removal of unused features, the remaining necessary applications may still contain exploitable vulnerabilities that this control does not directly mitigate (patch management and secure configuration are separate).\n4. Zero-day vulnerabilities in required, intentionally-exposed applications are not prevented by this control.\n5. Implementation effectiveness depends on proper execution and ongoing maintenance of the inventory; poorly executed removal efforts may be circumvented or services may be re-enabled inadvertently.\n\nThe control prevents the technique for approximately 75-85% of attack vectors in a typical OT environment, as it eliminates most unnecessary exposure while acknowledging that some intentional exposure for business purposes will remain. This rating reflects that it is a highly effective foundational control against this technique but does not completely prevent all possible exploitation vectors."}
