2026-05-31 17:29:47.502 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0895, technique_name=Autorun Image, technique_description=Adversaries may leverage AutoRun functionality or scripts to execute malicious code. Devices configured to enable AutoRun functionality or legacy operating systems may be susceptible to abuse of these features to run malicious code stored on various forms of removeable media (i.e., USB, Disk Images [.ISO]). Commonly, AutoRun or AutoPlay are disabled in many operating systems configurations to mitigate against this technique. If a device is configured to enable AutoRun or AutoPlay, adversaries may execute code on the device by mounting the removable media to the device, either through physical or virtual means. This may be especially relevant for virtual machine environments where disk images may be dynamically mapped to a guest system on a hypervisor., control_name=Removal of Unused Applications and Features, control_description=Removal of unused applications and features helps reduce overall attack surface by removing any possible attack vectors from applications and features whose presence is not necessary to fulfil the business purpose. In OT/ICS environments, this is accomplished by conducting a thorough inventory of all software components, enabled services, and communication protocols on each asset — including engineering workstations, HMIs, historians, and field devices — and systematically disabling or uninstalling anything not required for the device's defined operational function. This is particularly important in OT/ICS contexts because removing an unneeded component entirely is far more reliable than depending on timely patch deployment to remediate its vulnerabilities, and an unused service or legacy protocol left enabled represents an exploitable weakness with no operational justification.}
2026-05-31 17:29:52.032 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"This control provides a high degree of prevention against the AutoRun Image technique. By systematically removing unused applications and features from OT/ICS assets, this control directly addresses the core vulnerability that the technique exploits. Specifically: (1) AutoRun/AutoPlay functionality, when not required for operational purposes, would be disabled or removed entirely through this control's inventory and removal process; (2) unnecessary removable media drivers and related services that enable the attack vector would be eliminated; (3) virtual machine configurations would be hardened by removing disk image mounting capabilities unless explicitly required. The control is particularly effective because it removes the attack surface at the source rather than relying on runtime detection or user behavior. However, the rating is 4 rather than 5 because: (1) some OT environments may legitimately require AutoRun or removable media functionality for operational updates or configuration management, limiting universal applicability; (2) the control's effectiveness depends on thorough and accurate inventorying of all systems and accurate determination of what is 'unused' - misclassification could leave vulnerable functionality enabled; (3) in virtualized environments, hypervisor-level disk image mounting capabilities may still be available to attackers with elevated privileges, even if guest OS AutoRun is disabled; (4) the control requires ongoing maintenance to ensure disabled features remain disabled through patches and system updates. This control would successfully prevent the technique for the majority of OT/ICS deployments in typical configurations where AutoRun is not operationally necessary."}
