2026-06-14 11:48:37.376 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":1,"coverage_justification":"Session locking provides minimal protection against the Transient Cyber Asset technique. While session locking can prevent unauthorized access to an unattended workstation during inactivity, it does not address the core vulnerability exploited by this technique: the inherent trust and connectivity that transient assets possess when moving between external and internal ICS networks. Adversaries targeting transient cyber assets typically focus on: (1) compromising the device while it is on external networks before it enters the ICS environment, (2) leveraging the device's trusted credentials and access when it connects to internal systems, and (3) exploiting applications and libraries installed on the transient asset. Session locking only prevents access to a locked session on a single device and does nothing to prevent: malware pre-installed on a transient asset, credential theft from a compromised transient device, exploitation of vulnerable applications on the transient asset, or lateral movement using the transient asset's trusted access. An attacker who has compromised a transient asset before it enters the ICS environment can simply wait for the session lock to expire or reboot the device to regain access. The control is architecturally irrelevant to the fundamental attack vector of using a trusted but compromised transient device as a pivot point into the ICS network. It may only marginally slow an attacker if they rely on physically accessing an unlocked transient device in real-time, which is not the primary attack mechanism for this technique."}
2026-06-14 12:36:57.394 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0857, technique_name=System Firmware, technique_description=System firmware on modern assets is often designed with an update feature. Older device firmware may be factory installed and require special reprograming equipment. When available, the firmware update feature enables vendors to remotely patch bugs and perform upgrades. Device firmware updates are often delegated to the user and may be done using a software update package. It may also be possible to perform this task over the network. An adversary may exploit the firmware update feature on accessible devices to upload malicious or out-of-date firmware. Malicious modification of device firmware may provide an adversary with root access to a device, given firmware is one of the lowest programming abstraction layers., control_name=Secure boot, control_description=Secure Boot is a foundational security mechanism embedded in a device's hardware and firmware that ensures only cryptographically signed, trusted software is allowed to execute during the startup process. By systematically verifying the digital signatures of the bootloader, operating system kernel, and underlying firmware against a hardware root of trust, Secure Boot prevents attackers from loading unauthorised code, rootkits, or bootkits onto critical industrial assets like Programmable Logic Controllers (PLCs), Remote Terminal Units (RTUs), and edge gateways. This guarantees that the device boots into a known-good state before any industrial logic begins, effectively neutralising deeply persistent, low-level malware that could otherwise silently manipulate physical processes while evading standard operating system defences.}
2026-06-14 12:37:02.092 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Secure Boot provides a high degree of prevention against malicious firmware modifications by enforcing cryptographic signature verification of firmware and bootloader code before execution. This directly addresses the core threat vector of T0857, which involves uploading malicious firmware to devices. By establishing a hardware root of trust and validating digital signatures against this root, Secure Boot prevents unauthorized or tampered firmware from executing, even if an adversary successfully uploads malicious code to the device. This coverage is particularly effective against adversaries attempting to gain root access through firmware manipulation, as the firmware must pass signature verification before it can boot and execute. However, the rating of 4 rather than 5 accounts for several edge cases and limitations: (1) Secure Boot can be bypassed or disabled on some devices if physical access is available or if the implementation has flaws; (2) firmware update mechanisms themselves may have vulnerabilities that allow injection before signature verification occurs; (3) some legacy OT devices may not support Secure Boot or may have it disabled for operational compatibility reasons; (4) the control does not prevent all attack vectors, such as attacks that compromise the signing infrastructure or exploit zero-day vulnerabilities in the boot process itself; (5) in heterogeneous OT environments, not all critical assets may have Secure Boot properly implemented or enabled. Despite these limitations, when properly deployed and maintained across an ICS environment, Secure Boot is highly effective at preventing the successful execution of maliciously modified firmware, stopping the majority of standard and skilled attack attempts at this technique."}
